ACT ON INTEGRATED SUPPORT FOR COMMUNITY CARE, INCLUDING MEDICAL AND LONG-TERM CARE

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Act is to contribute to maintaining and promoting the healthy and dignified lives of the people by prescribing matters necessary to provide integrated and coordinated medical care, long-term care, and other support services so that persons who have difficulty performing daily activities due to frailty, disability, disease, accidents, or other causes may continue to lead healthy lives in the places where they have been living.

Article 2 (Definitions)
The terms used in this Act are defined as follows:
1. "Integrated support" means the integrated provision, directly or through coordination, by the State and the heads of local governments under this Act, to persons eligible for integrated support under subparagraph 2, of services in the fields of healthcare, health management, long-term care, daily living care, housing, and other fields prescribed by Presidential Decree (hereinafter referred to as "healthcare and other services").
2. "Person eligible for integrated support" means an older person, person with a disability, or other person prescribed by Presidential Decree who has difficulty maintaining daily living and requires multiple forms of support because of frailty, disability, disease, accidents, or other causes (hereinafter referred to as "frailty, etc.").
3. "Integrated-support-related institution" means an individual, corporation, institution, organization, or other person prescribed by Presidential Decree that provides services for integrated support under Articles 15 through 19 to persons eligible for integrated support.

Article 3 (Relationship to Other Acts)
With respect to integrated support, this Act shall take precedence over other Acts in matters provided for in this Act.

Article 4 (Responsibilities of the State and Local Governments)
(1) The State and local governments shall endeavor to create conditions in which persons who have difficulty maintaining daily living due to frailty, etc. can lead healthy and dignified lives in the places where they have been living until the end of their lives.
(2) When establishing and implementing policies on integrated support, the heads of local governments shall endeavor to ensure the following:
1. Establish a support system capable of comprehensively providing necessary services, ranging from preventive health management to end-of-life care, according to the characteristics and needs of persons eligible for integrated support;
2. Establish a coordinated system for continuous, home-centered integrated support that enables persons eligible for integrated support to continue receiving necessary services without interruption in the communities where they have been living, even after admission to and discharge from medical institutions, social welfare facilities, or similar institutions as needed;
3. Create a sufficient and sustainable integrated-support ecosystem at the level of local living areas based on the participation of community residents;
4. Guarantee the right of self-determination of persons eligible for integrated support so that they may decide for themselves whether, to what extent, and how to use integrated support after receiving sufficient explanations regarding its content and methods of use;
5. Enhance the public nature of the provision of various integrated-support services;
6. Provide support and protection for family members and guardians who care for persons eligible for integrated support.
(3) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayors, Special Self-Governing City Mayors, Do Governors, and Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors") shall secure infrastructure and financial resources and provide necessary support to Sis, Guns, and Gus in order to establish and expand integrated-support systems within their respective jurisdictions.
(4) The State shall provide the administrative and financial support necessary for local governments to fulfill paragraphs (1) through (3).

CHAPTER II ESTABLISHMENT OF BASIC PLANS FOR INTEGRATED SUPPORT

Article 5 (Establishment and Implementation of Basic Plans for Integrated Support)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare shall establish and implement a basic plan for integrated support (hereinafter referred to as a "basic plan") every five years in consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors.
(2) A basic plan shall include the following:
1. Objectives and basic directions for implementing integrated-support policies;
2. Measures to expand integrated-support infrastructure and services;
3. Measures for coordination and cooperation with central administrative agencies, specialized institutions under Article 25, and integrated-support-related institutions;
4. Matters concerning the integrated-support delivery system;
5. Matters concerning the improvement of statutes and systems related to integrated support;
6. Measures to support local governments;
7. Training of specialized personnel;
8. Matters concerning the procurement and operation of financial resources to promote integrated support;
9. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Health and Welfare for integrated support.
(3) When establishing a basic plan, the Minister of Health and Welfare shall ensure that it is linked to the following plans:
1. The basic plan for social security under Article 16 of the Framework Act on Social Security;
2. The healthcare development plan under Article 15 of the Framework Act on Health and Medical Services.
(4) A basic plan shall be finalized following deliberation by the Committee on Low Birth Rate and Aging Society under Article 23 of the Framework Act on Low Birth Rate in an Aging Society. The same shall apply to amendments to important matters of a basic plan as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(5) Matters necessary for the establishment and implementation of basic plans, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 6 (Establishment and Implementation of Regional Plans)
(1) Mayors/Do Governors and the heads of Sis (including administrative city mayors under Article 11 (1) of the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City; hereinafter the same shall apply), Guns, and Gus (referring to the heads of autonomous Gus; hereinafter referred to as "heads of Sis/Guns/Gus") shall establish and implement an annual regional plan for integrated support (hereinafter referred to as a "regional plan") in accordance with the basic plan, and a regional plan shall include the following:
1. Organization and operation of the integrated-support delivery system;
2. Identification of persons eligible for integrated support and establishment of a support system;
3. Procurement and operation of funds for integrated support;
4. Measures for the balanced supply of public infrastructure and resources for integrated support;
5. Measures for coordination and cooperation between integrated-support-related departments and institutions;
6. Improvement of ordinances and systems related to integrated support;
7. Other matters deemed necessary for integrated support by the relevant Mayor/Do Governor or head of a Si/Gun/Gu.
(2) Each head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall establish a regional plan after hearing the opinions of relevant stakeholders, including local residents, and submit it to the relevant Mayor/Do Governor; the Mayor/Do Governor shall establish a regional plan for the relevant City/Do, including measures to support the submitted plans of Sis/Guns/Gus, and submit it to the Minister of Health and Welfare.
(3) If the Minister of Health and Welfare or a Mayor/Do Governor deems it necessary with respect to the contents of a regional plan, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor may recommend adjustments thereto to the relevant Mayor/Do Governor or head of a Si/Gun/Gu, respectively, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare. In such cases, the Minister of Health and Welfare may hear the opinions of the heads of relevant central administrative agencies.
(4) Other matters necessary for the establishment and implementation of regional plans shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 7 (Surveys of Actual Conditions)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may conduct a survey of actual conditions concerning integrated support every five years for the establishment of basic plans and other purposes, and may publish the results.
(2) Matters necessary for the contents and methods of surveys under paragraph (1) and publication of their results shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 8 (Cooperation in Establishing Plans)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, Mayors/Do Governors, and heads of Sis/Guns/Gus may request cooperation, including the provision of data, from relevant institutions, organizations, or other persons when necessary for establishing and implementing basic and regional plans.
(2) A relevant institution, organization, or other person receiving a request for cooperation under paragraph (1) shall comply with the request unless there is a special reason not to do so.

Article 9 (Evaluation of Implementation Performance)
(1) Each Mayor/Do Governor and head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall submit annually to the Minister of Health and Welfare the regional plan for the following year under Article 6 and the performance in implementing the regional plan for the current year; the Minister of Health and Welfare shall evaluate the performance of regional plans and report the results to the Committee on Low Birth Rate and Aging Society under Article 23 of the Framework Act on Low Birth Rate in an Aging Society.
(2) Matters necessary for submission of regional plans and implementation performance and for evaluation of such performance under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

CHAPTER III PROCEDURES FOR INTEGRATED SUPPORT

Article 10 (Applications, Identification, and Investigations)
(1) Persons requiring integrated support, their family members, and persons prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, including personnel in charge of duties at integrated-support-related institutions, may apply for integrated support to the head of the Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over their place of residence or other competent authority, in accordance with relevant statutes.
(2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may identify persons urgently requiring support as prescribed by Presidential Decree, including persons whose applications for long-term care benefits under the Long-Term Care Insurance Act for Older Persons or for activity support benefits under the Act on Activity Assistant Services for Persons with Disabilities have been rejected and persons recently discharged from medical institutions under Article 3 of the Medical Service Act, through coordination with integrated-support-related institutions and through independent investigations, information analysis, and other means, and may apply for integrated support on their behalf ex officio so that they are not excluded from integrated support. When identifying a person eligible for integrated support, the authority shall explain the contents of integrated support to the person and obtain the person's prior consent.
(3) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may have public officials under the head's jurisdiction or other personnel investigate the following matters concerning a person requiring integrated support, or require the applicant to submit data necessary for such investigation. The same shall apply where further investigation is necessary following an application ex officio under paragraph (2):
1. Personal particulars, household characteristics, and residential environment of the person requiring integrated support;
2. Status of the person's receipt of social security benefits;
3. The person's ability to perform daily activities and social activities and the person's physical and mental health;
4. The person's needs for integrated support;
5. Other matters necessary for integrated support as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
(4) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may entrust an investigation under paragraph (3) to a specialized institution under Article 25 or conduct it in coordination with an integrated-support-related institution.
(5) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall conduct an investigation at least once every year to identify persons eligible for integrated support and shall endeavor to provide information and publicity concerning integrated support to local residents.

Article 11 (Coordination of Services for Discharged Patients)
(1) When a person requiring integrated support who has been hospitalized or admitted to an institution falling under any of the following subparagraphs intends to be discharged or to leave the institution, the head of the institution shall endeavor, after obtaining consent as prescribed by relevant statutes, to notify the head of the Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the person's residence of the intended discharge or departure and related matters, and to inform the person about applying for integrated support under Article 10, so that the person may continue receiving integrated support after discharge or departure:
1. A medical institution under Article 3 (2) of the Medical Service Act;
2. A long-term care institution providing institutional care benefits under Article 23 (1) 2 of the Long-Term Care Insurance Act for Older Persons;
3. Other institutions or facilities prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
(2) Matters necessary for notification of discharge or departure under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 12 (Comprehensive Assessment)
(1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall comprehensively assess persons requiring integrated support who have applied or have been identified under Article 10 (1) or (2), taking into account their medical needs and needs for long-term care and other care, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and shall inform each person eligible for integrated support of the assessment results reflecting that person's needs and level of need.
(2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall periodically assess the condition of persons eligible for integrated support who have been assessed under paragraph (1).
(3) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may entrust assessments under paragraph (1) and periodic assessments under paragraph (2) to a specialized institution under Article 25.

Article 13 (Establishment of Individual Support Plans)
(1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall establish an individual support plan for each person eligible for integrated support, including the following matters, in accordance with relevant statutes, and shall inform that person of the plan: Provided, That a plan for supporting the person's healthcare and other services established under another statute may substitute for an individual support plan if it is comprehensive:
1. The contents, methods, quantities, periods, and providers of integrated support;
2. Methods of coordination among institutions, corporations, organizations, or facilities where at least two such entities provide integrated-support services;
3. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Health and Welfare.
(2) When establishing an individual support plan, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall provide the person concerned and the person's family with sufficient opportunities to express their opinions and may consult investigators under Article 10 (3) or (4) or relevant persons at integrated-support providers.
(3) Matters necessary for establishing individual support plans under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 14 (Provision of Integrated Support)
(1) Where an individual support plan has been established under Article 13, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall endeavor to provide the necessary integrated support or refer the person to an integrated-support-related institution so that the support may be provided.
(2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may provide to integrated-support-related institutions relevant information from investigation results under Article 10 (2) or (3), within the scope necessary for integrated support.
(3) An integrated-support-related institution receiving a referral under paragraph (1) shall determine whether and to what extent it will provide services, in accordance with relevant statutes, and notify the head of the Si/Gun/Gu of its decision. The same shall apply to changes to the decision.
(4) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall regularly monitor the status of integrated support provision and changes in the condition of persons eligible for integrated support and may, based on the monitoring results, amend individual support plans or adjust the integrated-support services provided in consultation with the providers.
(5) Matters necessary for coordinating integrated support under paragraph (1), the scope and methods of providing information under paragraph (2), and monitoring, amendments, and adjustments not provided for in paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

CHAPTER IV IMPLEMENTATION AND SUPPORT OF INTEGRATED-SUPPORT POLICIES

Article 15 (Healthcare)
The State and local governments shall endeavor to expand the following services in the field of healthcare and strengthen their coordination with other services in order to provide integrated support suited to the needs and circumstances of persons eligible for integrated support: <Amended on Sep. 20, 2024>
1. Medical services provided by physicians, dentists, and doctors of Korean medicine under Article 2 (2) 1 through 3 of the Medical Service Act at medical institutions and in the homes and social welfare facilities of persons eligible for integrated support;
2. Nursing services provided by nurses under Article 12 of the Nursing Act in the homes and social welfare facilities of persons eligible for integrated support;
3. Rehabilitation services for maintaining and restoring the physical, mental, and social functioning of persons eligible for integrated support;
4. Medical services provided by long-term care hospitals and similar institutions under Article 3 (2) 3 (d) of the Medical Service Act for patients requiring recovery after prolonged hospitalization or acute-phase treatment;
5. Hospice projects under Article 21 (1) of the Act on Hospice and Palliative Care and Decisions on Life-Sustaining Treatment for Patients at the End of Life;
6. Visiting oral healthcare;
7. Medication counseling provided by pharmacists under Article 2, subparagraph 2 of the Pharmaceutical Affairs Act at pharmacies and in the homes and social welfare facilities of persons eligible for integrated support;
8. Other services prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 16 (Health Management and Prevention)
(1) The State and local governments shall endeavor, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, to prevent or alleviate frailty, geriatric diseases, chronic diseases, disabilities, mental illnesses, and other conditions among persons eligible for integrated support; to support their daily living; and to manage their health.
(2) Local governments shall establish a foundation for integrated support, including by designating integrated home-visiting institutions, so that the health management, prevention, and other activities under paragraph (1) can be coordinated through multidisciplinary cooperation in medicine, nursing, welfare, and other fields.

Article 17 (Long-Term Care)
The State and local governments shall endeavor to expand the following services relating to support for the physical or household activities of persons eligible for integrated support due to frailty, etc. and strengthen their coordination with other services:
1. Projects for preventing geriatric diseases implemented by the State and local governments under Article 4 (1) of the Long-Term Care Insurance Act for Older Persons;
2. Home-based care benefits and institutional care benefits under Article 23 (1) of the Long-Term Care Insurance Act for Older Persons;
3. Other services prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 18 (Daily Living Care)
The State and local governments shall endeavor to expand the following services under relevant statutes and ordinances, taking into account the characteristics of each person eligible for integrated support, and strengthen their coordination with other services so that persons eligible for integrated support may maintain and improve their physical and mental functioning and carry out daily living activities independently:
1. Services supporting household activities, including meal preparation, to maintain independent living at home for persons eligible for integrated support;
2. Transportation support services necessary for visits by persons eligible for integrated support to healthcare institutions and other places;
3. Services relating to assistive devices that improve or supplement the physical and mental functioning of persons eligible for integrated support and facilitate daily activities;
4. Services whereby persons eligible for integrated support who cannot receive family care due to unavoidable circumstances attend an appropriate facility or institution during the daytime or nighttime and receive necessary assistance;
5. Services based on information and communications technology, including artificial intelligence and the Internet of Things, for checking the safety and health status of persons eligible for integrated support;
6. Housing support services, including securing or providing residential space or improving residential environments in consideration of the functioning and health status of persons eligible for integrated support;
7. Services supporting the smooth return to the community of persons eligible for integrated support who are discharged from medical institutions or leave social welfare facilities;
8. Tailored benefits guidance services under Article 22-2 of the Act on the Use and Provision of Social Security Benefits and Search for Eligible Beneficiaries;
9. Other social welfare services prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 19 (Support for Families and Other Caregivers)
The State and local governments shall endeavor to maintain and manage the physical and mental health of family members, guardians, and other persons who support persons eligible for integrated support or provide them with counseling, information, and necessary services.

CHAPTER V ESTABLISHMENT OF FOUNDATIONS FOR INTEGRATED SUPPORT

Article 20 (Integrated Support Councils)
(1) Mayors/Do Governors and heads of Sis/Guns/Gus shall establish integrated support councils to facilitate the smooth implementation of integrated support within their respective jurisdictions and strengthen coordination and cooperation with integrated-support-related institutions and others. In such cases, they may operate such councils jointly with local social security councils under Article 41 of the Act on the Use and Provision of Social Security Benefits and Search for Eligible Beneficiaries.
(2) Integrated support councils under paragraph (1) shall deliberate and provide advice on the following:
1. Establishment and evaluation of regional plans;
2. Implementation of measures for integrated support;
3. Coordination and cooperation with integrated-support-related institutions and others;
4. Other matters deemed necessary by the chairperson.
(3) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of integrated support councils, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be prescribed by ordinance.

Article 21 (Establishment and Operation of Dedicated Organizations)
(1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may establish an organization dedicated to integrated support.
(2) The dedicated organization under paragraph (1) shall cooperate with integrated-support-related institutions and others and perform the following duties relating to integrated support:
1. Management of implementation of individual support plans under Article 13;
2. Operation of a system for coordination, adjustment, monitoring, and other integrated support centered on persons eligible for integrated support;
3. Identification and provision of integrated-support services using local resources;
4. Consultation and exchange of duties with integrated-support-related institutions and departments;
5. Securing and coordinating budgets for the duties under subparagraphs 1 through 4;
6. Other duties deemed necessary by the head of the Si/Gun/Gu.
(3) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may establish organizations at public health centers and in Eups, Myeons, and Dongs to support integrated-support duties.
(4) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of dedicated organizations and others under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by ordinance.

Article 22 (Establishment and Operation of Integrated Support Information System)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may establish and operate a computerized information system (hereinafter referred to as the "integrated support information system") to efficiently process various data and information necessary for operating the integrated-support procedures under Articles 10 through 14, providing integrated-support services under Articles 15 through 19, and coordinating persons eligible for integrated support with institutions providing those services (hereinafter referred to as "integrated-support duties"), and to computerize the recording and management of such data and information.
(2) For the purpose of handling integrated-support duties, including applications for integrated support, identification and investigation of eligible persons, comprehensive assessments, and provision and coordination of integrated-support services under this Act, the Minister of Health and Welfare may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, local governments, the National Health Insurance Service and the Health Insurance Review and Assessment Service under the National Health Insurance Act, the National Pension Service under the National Pension Act, medical institutions under Article 3 of the Medical Service Act in which persons eligible for integrated support are hospitalized, social welfare facilities under Article 2 of the Social Welfare Services Act in which such persons reside, and integrated-support-related institutions to submit data prescribed by Presidential Decree concerning eligible persons, including housing and income data. A person who receives such a request shall comply unless there is a justifiable reason not to do so.
(3) The integrated support information system may be electronically linked to the following information systems. In such cases, data or information collected through the linkage shall be limited to what is necessary for integrated-support duties:
1. The social security information system under Article 37 (2) of the Framework Act on Social Security;
2. The social service information system under Article 24-2 (1) of the Act on the Use and Provision of Social Security Benefits and Search for Eligible Beneficiaries;
3. The regional healthcare information system under Article 5 (1) of the Regional Public Health Act;
4. The dementia information system under Article 13-2 (1) of the Dementia Management Act;
5. Information systems managed and operated by the National Health Insurance Service to perform duties relating to health insurance and long-term care insurance for older persons;
6. Other information systems prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for integrated-support duties, including applications for integrated support and identification of eligible persons.
(4) The Minister of Health and Welfare may entrust duties relating to the establishment and operation of the integrated support information system to relevant institutions or organizations, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
(5) Other matters necessary for the establishment and operation of the integrated support information system shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 23 (Provision and Use of Information)
(1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may request integrated-support-related institutions and specialized institutions under Article 25 to provide information necessary to perform integrated-support duties.
(2) Upon receiving a request under paragraph (1), integrated-support-related institutions and specialized institutions under Article 25 may process and provide to the head of the Si/Gun/Gu information concerning computerized resident registration records; computerized family relationship registration records; finance, national taxes, and local taxes; land and buildings; national health insurance, long-term care insurance, and medical benefits; the national pension, employment insurance, basic pension, and disability pension; health examinations; admission to and discharge from medical institutions; admission to and departure from social welfare facilities; occupancy of public housing; and other information prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) The heads of Sis/Guns/Gus, integrated-support-related institutions, and specialized institutions under Article 25 may mutually request the provision of information necessary within the scope of their respective duties under an individual support plan referred to in Article 13, including the name, residence, and contact information (including a guardian's contact information) of a person eligible for integrated support, the contents and providers of services that have been provided or are needed, and results of investigations under Article 10, in order to perform integrated-support duties smoothly. An institution receiving such a request shall comply unless there is a justifiable reason not to do so.
(4) Matters necessary for the types and scope of information under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 24 (Training of Specialized Personnel)
(1) The State and local governments shall devise measures necessary to train and secure specialized personnel for integrated support and improve their qualifications.
(2) To train specialized personnel for integrated support, the State may designate research institutes, universities, and other institutions deemed necessary as institutions for training and retraining specialized personnel.
(3) The Minister of Health and Welfare and the heads of central administrative agencies may subsidize all or part of the expenses necessary for training personnel at institutions designated under paragraph (2), within the budget.

Article 25 (Designation of Specialized Institutions)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may designate specialized institutions, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, to perform the following duties in order to fully and efficiently identify persons eligible for integrated support, collect, investigate, and analyze relevant data, and efficiently implement integrated-support policies:
1. Support for the establishment and publicity of integrated-support policies of the State and local governments and for evaluation of the performance of regional plans under Article 9;
2. Analysis of the characteristics and types of persons eligible for integrated support;
3. Support for identifying and investigating eligible persons under Article 10 and development of investigation standards for the matters under the subparagraphs of Article 10 (3);
4. Comprehensive assessments under Article 12;
5. Other matters necessary for integrated support as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
(2) The Minister of Health and Welfare may subsidize all or part of the expenses necessary to perform the duties under paragraph (1).

CHAPTER VI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 26 (Pilot Projects)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may conduct pilot projects for the effective implementation of the integrated-support system where difficulties are anticipated in implementing it in full or where there is a need to verify its methods of implementation in advance.
(2) Matters necessary for the persons eligible for pilot projects, the areas where they are implemented, and their methods shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Health and Welfare.

Article 27 (Maintenance of Confidentiality)
No person who is or has been engaged in duties under Articles 10 through 14 or Article 23 shall disclose to any other person confidential information learned in the course of such duties or use such information for any purpose other than the performance of the duties.

Article 28 (Financial Support and Cost Sharing)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may subsidize all or part of the expenses necessary for the implementation of integrated-support measures by Mayors/Do Governors and heads of Sis/Guns/Gus, within the budget. In such cases, subsidies may be differentiated by reflecting the evaluation results under Article 9.
(2) The Minister of Health and Welfare, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, Mayors/Do Governors, and heads of Sis/Guns/Gus may subsidize all or part of the expenses incurred by integrated-support-related institutions.
(3) Personnel and expenses necessary for integrated-support-related institutions may be supported under the National Health Insurance Act, the Long-Term Care Insurance Act for Older Persons, and other relevant statutes.
(4) The heads of Sis/Guns/Gus and integrated-support-related institutions who have borne expenses necessary for integrated support may require persons eligible for integrated support to bear all or part of such expenses in accordance with the standards prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 29 (Delegation of Authority and Entrustment of Duties)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may delegate part of the Minister's authority under this Act to the heads of local governments.
(2) The Minister of Health and Welfare and the heads of local governments may entrust part of the following duties concerning integrated support to specialized institutions under Article 25, integrated-support-related institutions, or other persons, as prescribed by Presidential Decree:
1. Evaluation of implementation performance of regional plans under Article 9;
2. Investigations under Article 10;
3. Establishment of individual support plans under Article 13;
4. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for the specialized implementation of integrated support.
(3) The Minister of Health and Welfare and the heads of local governments shall provide personnel or funds necessary for the performance of duties delegated or entrusted under paragraphs (1) and (2).

CHAPTER VII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 30 (Penalty Provisions)
A person who discloses confidential information learned in the course of duties to another person, or uses such information for a purpose other than the performance of duties, in violation of Article 27 shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than five years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won.

ADDENDA <Act No. 20415, Mar. 26, 2024>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Act shall enter into force two years after the date of its promulgation: Provided, That Article 26 shall enter into force on the date of promulgation.

Article 2 (Preparatory Acts for the Enforcement of This Act)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may take preparatory measures necessary for the enforcement of this Act, including establishing and operating the integrated support information system and establishing a basic plan, from the date of promulgation of this Act.
(2) Before this Act enters into force, Mayors/Do Governors and heads of Sis/Guns/Gus may make preparations necessary to establish and implement regional plans under Article 6.

ADDENDA (Nursing Act) <Act No. 20445, Sep. 20, 2024>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Act shall enter into force nine months after the date of its promulgation: Provided, That ... <Omitted> ..., Article 9 (8) of these Addenda shall enter into force on March 27, 2026.

Articles 2 through 8 Omitted.

Article 9 (Amendments to Other Acts)
